Done — `python3 demo.py` passes all three cases, and `evaluate` now handles a full mini-language instead of just those examples.

Implementation in [filterlang.py](/Users/eranshir/Documents/Projects/deepseek/bench/hard/work/deepseek_h4_underspec_t2/filterlang.py:303): a hand-rolled tokenizer plus recursive-descent parser (stdlib only, no eval/exec), with the assumed grammar documented in the module docstring since the ticket had none.

What it supports:

- Logic: `and`, `or`, `not` (case-insensitive, standard precedence `not` > `and` > `or`), and parentheses for grouping.
- Comparisons: `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, plus `=` as an alias for `==`.
- Membership: `tags contains 'vip'` (substring for strings, item lookup for lists/sets, key lookup for dicts) and the reversed `value in [...]` form.
- Literals: integers, floats (including `-2`, `1e3`), single/double-quoted strings with escapes, `true`/`false` in any case, and `None`/`null`.
- Fields: bare identifiers, including hyphenated names and dotted paths into nested dicts (`user.age`), with exact-match-first then case-insensitive lookup.

A few judgment calls worth flagging, since the ticket was silent:

- A missing field resolves to `None`, so `not banned` is `True` when the field is absent, and ordering comparisons against missing fields return `False`.
- Type mismatches (e.g. `age > 30` when `age` is `"old"`) return `False` rather than crashing, so one bad record can't break a segment run.
- Malformed expressions raise `ValueError` with position info so the web form can surface authoring mistakes — the one behavior that was genuinely ambiguous, so easy to change if you'd prefer `False`.

I verified it against 48 valid expressions (precedence, parens, missing fields, nested dicts, Unicode/Hebrew keys, string escapes, `in`/`contains` variants) plus malformed-input checks, all passing.
